SYNOPSIS
        void async_write(string file, string text, int flag, closure callback)

DESCRIPTION
        Queue text for background file writing. This efun is available only
        when the driver is configured with --enable-use-async-io.

        flag 0 appends; flag 1 truncates and overwrites. Other flags are an
        error. The master valid_write() operation is "write_file" and the
        H_FILE_ENCODING hook supplies the encoding, as for write_file().
        Conversion, including its final shift sequence, happens during the
        call. Embedded NUL characters in text are preserved.

        callback receives one integer: 0 on success, -1 on an I/O or helper
        failure. It runs later from the backend, never inside async_write().
        Its command giver is captured at submission. A destroyed callback
        target is skipped; the accepted write still happens. Errors raised
        by a callback do not prevent later completions.

        Permission, argument, conversion, allocation and queue-limit errors
        are raised synchronously. Such a request is not accepted, touches no
        destination, and has no callback. Calls also fail synchronously if
        the helper is unavailable or orderly shutdown is draining the queue.

        async_write() and async_save_object() share one FIFO. Accepted file
        operations and callbacks are ordered by publication, including writes
        to different paths. A request submitted recursively by a permission
        or encoding hook is published before its outer request. Existing
        synchronous efuns and external writers do not participate in this
        ordering. Wait for completion before rotating a log or synchronously
        replacing or reading an asynchronously written file.

        Preparation and an immutable byte copy remain on the driver thread.
        A separate helper performs the open, write and close operations.
        The default limits are 1024 outstanding requests, 64 MiB outstanding
        payload capacity, and 16 MiB in one request. In-flight requests count
        against those limits. Buffer capacity is counted conservatively.

        Append and overwrite failures may leave a partially written file.
        Requests are never automatically replayed. A lost helper reply can
        mean that a mutation completed but its result is unknown. The helper
        remains unavailable until the driver is restarted.

        Success requires a complete write and successful close. It does not
        imply fsync or survival of power loss. On orderly shutdown, the
        master notify_shutdown() hook can queue final writes; admission then
        closes and pending operations drain with a configurable deadline
        (30 seconds by default). A drain failure makes an otherwise successful
        exit nonzero. Callback evaluation time is additional to the I/O wait
        deadline and is subject to the normal evaluation limit.

EXAMPLES
        void logged(int result)
        {
            if (result) debug_message("Log write failed.\n");
        }

        async_write("log/events", "Player connected.\n", 0, #'logged);

